hivalik ridges.</li><li>The dam site can be reached on the road between Mandi and Bilaspur, and the reservoir has already attracted winter waterfowl.</li><li>The project's powerhouse is built underground within the gorge walls — an engineering approach that minimises surface impact.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.55,76.65&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.55,76.65" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Nalwari Fair Ground</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Nalwari Mela, held annually in March at the Bilaspur Chaugan (town maidan), is one of the largest traditional cattle and livestock fairs in Himachal Pradesh — an event of considerable antiquity that historically served as the primary market for livestock trade between the hill communities and the plains buyers.</li><li>While modern commercial livestock markets have reduced the scale of actual cattle trade, the fair has expanded as a cultural event with folk performances, food stalls, handicraft markets and agricultural equipment displays.</li><li>The March timing coincides with the post-winter agricultural opening, and the fair has a distinctly festive atmosphere.</li><li>The event runs for 3–5 days and draws tens of thousands of visitors.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.34,76.75&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.34,76.75" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Vyas Gufa</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>On a rocky promontory above the Satluj river within Bilaspur town, the Vyas Gufa (Cave of Vyasa) is a natural cave associated with the legendary sage Vyas — credited with composing or assembling the Mahabharata and the Vedas.</li><li>The cave has been a place of meditation for ascetics over many generations, and the current temple structure surrounding it has been maintained by a continuous succession of priests.</li><li>The cave survived the Bhakra submergence because it sits above the full reservoir level.</li><li>The Satluj gorge view from the cave entrance — the river 40 m below, the Gobind Sagar reservoir stretching south, and the Shivalik hills receding in every direction — is one of the district's finest viewpoints.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.35,76.76&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.35,76.76" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Dehar Hydro</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>430 m</span><span>38 km trans-Himalayan tunnel at Sundernagar</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Dehar Power House on the Beas-Satluj Link Project, located where the diverted Beas water emerges from the 38 km trans-Himalayan tunnel at Sundernagar, is an underground power station buried within the hill — its turbines driven by a 430 m head of water from the Pandoh intake.</li><li>The exterior of the facility is modest, but the engineering concept — the water travelling more than 38 km underground to drop 430 m and generate power — is extraordinary.</li><li>The Dehar facility is not a tourist site per se, but the approach road along the Satluj between Bilaspur and Sundernagar passes through genuinely wild gorge country with good views of the river and the Shivalik formations.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.5,76.6&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.5,76.6" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Swarghat</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>Where the Satluj river enters Gobind Sagar at its northern end near Swarghat, the meeting of a fast mountain river with the still reservoir creates an exceptional landscape.</li><li>The Satluj in its final gorge section above this point cuts through narrow sandstone cliffs that are coloured in bands of red, orange and buff — the characteristic Shivalik geological palette.</li><li>A river beach at the confluence point is used for picnicking and casual fishing by local families.</li><li>The Swarghat bridge on the Bilaspur-Mandi highway is a viewpoint for watching the Satluj emerge from its gorge.</li><li>In October and November, when the reservoir is drawn down and the river is still running clear, the colour contrast between the blue-green river and the red cliffs is photogenic.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.55,76.75&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.55,76.75" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div></div></div>
