dius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=32.0994,76.2692&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=32.0994,76.2692" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Triund Trek</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>2,828 m</span><span>9 km trail from McLeod Ganj that ascends through oak and rhododendron forest before emerging on a long grassy ridge directly beneath the Dhauladhar's vertical rock face</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Triund ridge (2,828 m) is accessed by a 9 km trail from McLeod Ganj that ascends through oak and rhododendron forest before emerging on a long grassy ridge directly beneath the Dhauladhar's vertical rock face.</li><li>The contrast is abrupt and spectacular: the trail that passes through dense forest delivers walkers without warning to an open meadow with a sheer 1,500 m cliff rising immediately behind and the entire Kangra Valley spread 1,400 m below.</li><li>The trek takes 3–4 hours ascending, 2–3 hours descending, and is suitable for reasonably fit walkers.</li><li>Overnight camping on the ridge, in tents rented from Gaddi shepherd families, transforms the experience — dawn light on the Dhauladhar is memorable.</li><li>The Snow Line Café at the ridge serves chai and dal; beyond it, the trail continues to Lahesh Cave and the Indrahar Pass (4,342 m).</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=32.27,76.35&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=32.27,76.35" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Masrur Rock Cut Temple</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>Carved in the early 8th century from a single massive sandstone outcrop in the Beas valley, the Masrur temples are among the most remarkable and least-visited heritage sites in northern India — a group of 15 interconnected shikharas cut directly into a natural hillside in the late Pratihara style.</li><li>Partially destroyed by the 1905 earthquake and never fully excavated, the temples contain carved panels of Shiva, Vishnu and their attendants of considerable artistic refinement.</li><li>The reservoir created below the rock reflects The temple site at sunset, creating a photographic composition particularly scenic.</li><li>Masrur is often compared to the Ellora caves and sometimes called 'the Kangra Khajuraho'; This place is inexplicably absent from most tourist itineraries.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=32.0511,76.4242&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=32.0511,76.4242" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Brajeshwari Temple</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>Immediately adjacent to the Kangra Fort on the same rocky outcrop, the Brajeshwari Devi temple is one of the 51 Shakti Peethas — the sacred sites marking where the body of the goddess Sati fell according to Puranic tradition.</li><li>The temple was demolished by Mahmud of Ghazni in 1009 CE, and rebuilt multiple times; the 1905 earthquake destroyed the current structure's predecessor.</li><li>The temple receives especially large numbers of pilgrims during the Navratri festivals (March–April and September–October).</li><li>The gold-plated shikhara, rebuilt after 1905, gleams above the surrounding old bazaar of Kangra town, which retains its traditional street of cloth merchants and sweets shops.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=32.0989,76.2696&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=32.0989,76.2696" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><img src="https://drive.google.com/thumbnail?id=1wd5un96nu3bUfGxgHnV4KOiHC_oa7G1Z&sz=w600" alt="Kareri Lake" loading="lazy"><h3>Kareri Lake</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>2,950 m</span><span>14 km trail from Dharamshala winds through dense oak and rhododendron forest</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>A 14 km trail from Dharamshala winds through dense oak and rhododendron forest, crossing the Kareri stream multiple times on rope bridges and stepping stones before reaching this glacial lake at 2,950 m directly beneath the Dhauladhar's northern face.</li><li>The lake freezes completely in winter and is surrounded by snow on its northern shore until late May.</li><li>Trout have been introduced and the clear water supports a healthy population.</li><li>Camping is permitted on the flat ground at the lake's eastern shore; basic food is available from a seasonal dhaba.</li><li>The continuation trail climbs to the Minkiani Pass (4,350 m) for experienced trekkers with high-altitude gear.</li><li>Kareri is a full-day excursion from Dharamshala or a more satisfying 2-day trip with a night camping at the lake.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=32.3,76.32&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=32.3,76.32" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Dharamkot</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>A kilometre above McLeod Ganj on a forested spur, Dharamkot has evolved into Himachal's most concentrated centre for yoga, meditation and Vipassana practice.</li><li>Dozens of centres offer courses ranging from drop-in yoga classes to month-long residential programmes in the Tibetan Buddhist, Vipassana, Kundalini and Ashtanga traditions.</li><li>The village has strong cafes, a small traveller library and guesthouses in converted apple orchards.</li><li>The trail to Triund begins here, and the short walk to the Guna Devi temple viewpoint offers the same Kangra Valley panorama without the full trek commitment.</li><li>Dharamkot attracts a throughout the year community of long-stay international visitors seeking study and practice in an unhurried mountain setting.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=32.23,76.32&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=32.23,76.32" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Chamunda Devi</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>Set on a cliff above the Baner river gorge, the Chamunda Devi temple ranks among the most revered Shakti shrines in Himachal Pradesh — the fierce goddess in her form as destroyer of the demons Chanda and Munda is worshipped here in a form considered particularly powerful.</li><li>The temple site, rebuilt in the mid-20th century on an ancient site, receives large numbers of pilgrims throughout the year, swelling enormously at Navratri.</li><li>The gorge viewpoint adjacent to the temple, where the river has cut a narrow canyon through the Shivalik foothills, is atmospheric.</li><li>The town of Chamunda, below the temple, has a traditional bazaar and several dharamshalas for pilgrims.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=32.1833,76.45&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=32.1833,76.45" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div></div></div>
