, or by a rough road passable in summer.</li><li>In winter (December–February) the approach trail is under 2–3 m of snow and the temple is completely buried; pilgrims then descend and perform puja at the treeline.</li><li>The summThis place is on a major migratory bird flyway and is outstanding for raptor watching in September–October.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.75,77.05&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.75,77.05" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Barot Valley</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,800 m</span><span>53 km tunnel to carry the Uhl's water to powerhouses in the Kangra Valley</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The valley of the Uhl river at Barot village (1,800 m) was shaped by the Shanan hydroelectric project of 1932 — the oldest major hydroelectric installation in India still in operation, built by the British using a 53 km tunnel to carry the Uhl's water to powerhouses in the Kangra Valley.</li><li>The British-era bungalows, stone bridges and the spillway infrastructure give Barot a curiously timeless quality.</li><li>The Uhl river is one of Himachal's premier brown trout fisheries (Forest Department permits available in Barot).</li><li>Camping in the pine forest above the river and the walk to the Barot Lake through rhododendron forest are the main activities.</li><li>The valley connects via the Jalori Pass area to the Tirthan Valley and Kullu district.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=32.05,76.85&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=32.05,76.85" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Jogindernagar</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>165 km</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The western terminus of the Kangra Valley Railway — the narrow-gauge 'toy train' line built between 1926 and 1929 that links Pathankot to this small town through the Kangra Valley — Jogindernagar is primarily a functional base for the Shanan hydro project whose penstocks descend from the Barot tunnel.</li><li>The Uhl river below town offers river rafting, and the Ravi river confluence zone to the north has good bird habitat.</li><li>The Kangra Valley Railway journey from Pathankot (165 km, 8–9 hours) is one of the great scenic railway experiences of India, passing through rice paddies, tea gardens, bamboo groves and small-town markets on a line that UNESCO has recognised as part of India's mountain railway heritage.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.9983,76.7833&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.9983,76.7833" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><img src="https://drive.google.com/thumbnail?id=1-sp6Cw6BF4aS5aYdesXZmUKLnX2E-qV4&sz=w600" alt="Sundernagar" loading="lazy"><h3>Sundernagar</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,155 m</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The largest sub-divisional town of Mandi district, Sundernagar at 1,155 m on the Satluj sits above the Sukh Sagar Lake — a reservoir of the Beas-Satluj Link Project that has created a significant water body in the Mandi lowlands.</li><li>The town has a college, a government hospital and a busy market, and serves as the practical hub for lower Mandi district travel.</li><li>The Shri Naina Devi temple complex on the ridge above the lake offers panoramic views.</li><li>Sundernagar is the junction town for travellers entering Mandi from Chandigarh on the NH3 — the faster Beas river valley route to Manali passes through here.</li><li>The Sukh Sagar lake shore now includes with gardens and a boating facility.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.5292,76.9&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.5292,76.9" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><img src="https://drive.google.com/thumbnail?id=1dnb8e0Zh3YO0nLzL6MrzbdVm-OGmIOIs&sz=w600" alt="Kamlah Fort" loading="lazy"><h3>Kamlah Fort</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>4 km from Mandi town is a scenic if fragmentary heritage site — its stone walls</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The ruined fort of the former Sukhet kingdom on a rocky hillock 4 km from Mandi town is a scenic if fragmentary heritage site — its stone walls, gateways and water cisterns now largely reclaimed by grass and wild fig trees.</li><li>The Sukhet kingdom was an independent polity that controlled central Mandi before being absorbed by the Mandi rajas in the 18th century.</li><li>The fort views over the Beas valley and toward the Dhauladhar are excellent.</li><li>A small temple on the summit ridge is still active.</li><li>The climb from the base village takes approximately 45 minutes on a rough trail through scrub forest.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.7,77&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.7,77" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Pandoh Dam</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>15 km south of Kullu town</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Pandoh Dam on the Beas river, 15 km south of Kullu town, is the intake structure of the Beas-Satluj Link Project — a major engineering achievement that diverts Beas water through a 38 km tunnel under the Dhauladhar to the Satluj at Sundernagar.</li><li>The reservoir created behind the dam fills a narrow gorge with exceptionally clear green water, flanked by near-vertical cliff faces.</li><li>A viewpoint and picnic area on the dam top attracts local visitors.</li><li>The road on the south bank of the reservoir, bypassed by the main NH3 highway, is a scenic alternative with fine views of the water in the gorge.</li><li>The dam has eliminated the spring floods that historically devastated the lower Beas valley.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.68,77.02&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.68,77.02" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Karsog</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,400 m</span><span>40 km detour from the main highway</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>Hidden in a broad sub-valley of the Satluj system south of the main Mandi-Shimla highway, Karsog at 1,400 m is one of the least-visited valley settlements in Mandi district — a landscape of terraced rice fields, apple orchards and deodar forest with a distinctly traditional Himachali character.</li><li>The Mahunag temple fair (July–August) is one of the district's most important local festivals, drawing devotees from surrounding villages who walk for hours through the forest.</li><li>The apple harvest (September–October) is the best time to visit; the orchards around Karsog produce a unique 'Karsog apple' variety prized for its flavour.</li><li>A handful of basic guesthouses and homestays accommodate the occasional traveller who takes the 40 km detour from the main highway.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.3833,77.2&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.3833,77.2" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div></div></div>
