-  <div class="section" id="places"><h2>Top Places to Visit (10)</h2><div class="places-grid"><div class="place-card"><h3>Renuka Lake</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>3,214 m</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The largest natural lake in Himachal Pradesh at 3,214 m length and up to 672 m width, Renuka is shaped like the reclining form of the goddess Renuka — the mother of the warrior-sage Parashurama — and this resemblance is the source of its sacred identity.</li><li>A smaller lake adjacent to the main body is said to be Parashurama himself, watching over his mother.</li><li>The annual Renuka Fair (November–December, at the Kartik Purnima full moon) is one of Himachal's most important religious gatherings, drawing over 200,000 pilgrims over five days.</li><li>The lakeside has a modest zoo with a lion, tiger and leopard enclosure, a boating facility, and walking paths.</li><li>The surrounding forest is habitat for leopard and sloth bear.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.6089,77.4532&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.6089,77.4532" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Churdhar Peak</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>3,647 m</span><span>14 km trek from Nohradhar or Haripurdhar for darshan</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>At 3,647 m, Churdhar is the highest peak of the outer Himalayan range — the Shivalik system — and the highest point reachable by trekking in the districts of Sirmour and Sirmaur without crossing into the inner ranges.</li><li>The Shirgul Maharaj temple on the summThis place is served by a priest who stays from May to October; pilgrims from the Sirmour and Shimla foothills complete the 14 km trek from Nohradhar or Haripurdhar for darshan.</li><li>The route passes through remarkable forest transitions: subtropical thorn scrub at the base gives way to mixed subtropical forest, then oak and rhododendron, then silver fir and finally the open alpine zone near the summit.</li><li>The 360° panorama from the top extends from the Shivalik plains to the Kinnaur Kailash range.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.8394,77.5094&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.8394,77.5094" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Nahan</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>932 m</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>Founded in 1621 by Raja Karam Prakash of the Sirmour kingdom on a commanding ridge at 932 m, Nahan is one of Himachal Pradesh's finest examples of a planned hill town — its street grid, central market square and palace complex were laid out in a single design that remains largely intact.</li><li>The Rani Tal Garden, created by a Sirmour queen in the 19th century as a private pleasure garden, is now a public park of great charm.</li><li>The Jagannath temple complex on the town ridge has been an active pilgrimage site for four centuries.</li><li>Nahan's October–March climate — mild days and cool nights, minimal rainfall — makes it the most comfortable lowland town in Himachal and an underrated base for exploring southern Sirmour.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.5583,77.2989&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.5583,77.2989" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Suketi Fossil Park</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>Asia's first fossil park, established in 1974 at Suketi on the banks of the Markanda river, preserves evidence of the extraordinary Shivalik fossil fauna that inhabited this region between 5 and 8 million years ago when the foothills were open woodland and savannah.</li><li>Life-size concrete replicas of Stegodon (an ancient elephant relative), Sivatherium (a giant giraffe), the Shivalik hippopotamus and a Sivapithecus primate are displayed at the sites where their original fossils were excavated.</li><li>The adjoining fossil museum contains actual specimens including a complete Stegodon skull.</li><li>The landscape along the Markanda here is representative of the lower Shivalik — gravel river bars, scrub forest and the red-and-buff Siwalik geological formations.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.67,77.38&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.67,77.38" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Paonta Sahib</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>On the west bank of the Yamuna river at the Himachal Pradesh–Uttarakhand border, Paonta Sahib ranks among the most significant Sikh historical sites — the gurdwara marks the place where Guru Gobind Singh, the 10th Sikh Guru, lived from 1685 to 1689 and composed much of his literary work including the Dasam Granth.</li><li>The Langar (free community kitchen) at the gurdwara feeds thousands daily.</li><li>The Yamuna at Paonta Sahib is broad and calm in comparison to its upper reaches, and the riverside setting is pleasant.</li><li>The town is a significant pharmaceutical manufacturing centre — the 'pharma valley' around Paonta Sahib is among India's larger drug manufacturing clusters, a fact that gives the district an unusual economic character.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.4367,77.6267&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.4367,77.6267" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Haripurdhar</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>2,100 m</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>At approximately 2,100 m on the ridge above the Sirmour–Shimla watershed, Haripurdhar is the most accessible base for the Churdhar trek — typically a 2-day journey to the summit and back.</li><li>The village has a small bazaar, a primary health centre and basic guesthouses that fill with pilgrims in the summer trekking season.</li><li>The apple orchards around Haripurdhar produce good quality fruit in September–October.</li><li>The trail to Churdhar through the silver fir forest above the village is well-marked and in good condition in dry weather.</li><li>The ridge viewpoints above the village, accessible on shorter walks, offer views toward both the Shimla hills and the Sirmour plains.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.8,77.52&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.8,77.52" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Rajgarh</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,600 m</span><span>60 km)</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Pabbar river valley between Rohru (Shimla district) and the Sirmour border passes through Rajgarh — a small administrative centre at approximately 1,600 m that is the commercial hub for the apple orchards filling this section of the valley.</li><li>The Pabbar here is an excellent brown trout fishery with a Forest Department permit system.</li><li>The valley landscape in October, with the apple harvest underway and the surrounding deciduous trees turning colour, is exceptionally scenic and almost entirely unvisited by tourists.</li><li>A road connects Rajgarh across the watershed to Renuka Lake (approximately 60 km), making a circuit possible for those with their own vehicle.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31,77.3667&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31,77.3667" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Sarahan (Sirmour)</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>Not to be confused with the more famous Sarahan in Kinnaur, this Sarahan in Sirmour district is a small village on the Girri river where the ancient Trilokpur Mata temple draws devotees from across the Shivalik foothills.</li><li>The temple, set in a forested gorge, is an important local pilgrimage centre for Sirmour's Hindu communities.</li><li>The Girri valley here is typical of the lower Sirmour landscape — narrow, humid and forested with subtropical species including bamboo and wild banana at lower elevations.</li><li>This area is undeveloped for tourism and largely visited by pilgrims rather than travellers.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.52,77.57&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.52,77.57" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Dadahu</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>A small market village on the Giri Ganga river in central Sirmour, Dadahu is primarily visited as a river camping base — the Giri Ganga in this section runs clear and swift through a forested gorge that is good for nature walks and seasonal fishing.</li><li>The surrounding oak forest supports leopard, barking deer and a variety of woodland birds.</li><li>The October–March period, when the weather is mild and clear and the forest is dry, is ideal for camp-based exploration.</li><li>Basic accommodation is available in the village; most visitors camp on the river flats.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.5833,77.4333&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.5833,77.4333" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Shillai</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>90 km) through continuous forest — the journey itself is as rewarding as the destination</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>On the Uttarakhand border at the southeastern tip of Sirmour district, Shillai is a remote sub-divisional headquarters in a landscape of dense oak and rhododendron forest that is among the least-visited terrain in Himachal Pradesh.</li><li>The terrain here transitions from the Himalayan foothills to the outer Garhwal ranges, and the forest is remarkably intact — leopard, goral, barking deer and the sloth bear are all present.</li><li>The spring wildflower display (March–May) in the oak and rhododendron forest below the ridge is spectacular and entirely undiscovered by tourism.</li><li>Shillai can be reached on a road from Paonta Sahib (approximately 90 km) through continuous forest — the journey itself is as rewarding as the destination.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.7167,77.7333&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.7167,77.7333" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div></div></div>
