gle.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31,77.3667" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><img src="https://drive.google.com/thumbnail?id=1lJbRMTasmTMQsiKWWXovV2s9wI3J_KbU&sz=w600" alt="Sarahan (Sirmour)" loading="lazy"><h3>Sarahan (Sirmour)</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>Not to be confused with the more famous Sarahan in Kinnaur, this Sarahan in Sirmour district is a small village on the Girri river where the ancient Trilokpur Mata temple draws devotees from across the Shivalik foothills.</li><li>The temple, set in a forested gorge, is an important local pilgrimage centre for Sirmour's Hindu communities.</li><li>The Girri valley here is typical of the lower Sirmour landscape — narrow, humid and forested with subtropical species including bamboo and wild banana at lower elevations.</li><li>This area is undeveloped for tourism and largely visited by pilgrims rather than travellers.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.52,77.57&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.52,77.57" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Dadahu</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>A small market village on the Giri Ganga river in central Sirmour, Dadahu is primarily visited as a river camping base — the Giri Ganga in this section runs clear and swift through a forested gorge that is good for nature walks and seasonal fishing.</li><li>The surrounding oak forest supports leopard, barking deer and a variety of woodland birds.</li><li>The October–March period, when the weather is mild and clear and the forest is dry, is ideal for camp-based exploration.</li><li>Basic accommodation is available in the village; most visitors camp on the river flats.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.5833,77.4333&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.5833,77.4333" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Shillai</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>90 km) through continuous forest — the journey itself is as rewarding as the destination</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>On the Uttarakhand border at the southeastern tip of Sirmour district, Shillai is a remote sub-divisional headquarters in a landscape of dense oak and rhododendron forest that is among the least-visited terrain in Himachal Pradesh.</li><li>The terrain here transitions from the Himalayan foothills to the outer Garhwal ranges, and the forest is remarkably intact — leopard, goral, barking deer and the sloth bear are all present.</li><li>The spring wildflower display (March–May) in the oak and rhododendron forest below the ridge is spectacular and entirely undiscovered by tourism.</li><li>Shillai can be reached on a road from Paonta Sahib (approximately 90 km) through continuous forest — the journey itself is as rewarding as the destination.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.7167,77.7333&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.7167,77.7333" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div></div></div>
