-  <div class="section" id="places"><h2>Top Places to Visit (10)</h2><div class="places-grid"><div class="place-card"><h3>Kasauli</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>2,455 m</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>Established in 1842 as a British military sanitarium, Kasauli is among the best-preserved hill cantonments in India — a living museum of colonial Himachal where the architecture, the tree-lined lanes and even the pace of life seem largely unchanged from the late Victorian era.</li><li>The Kasauli Cantonment Board maintains strict rules: no vehicles are permitted in the inner Mall area, no new construction can exceed the old style, and the original colonial bungalows must be maintained.</li><li>Monkey Point at the cantonment's southern tip (2,455 m), accessible on foot or in a cantonment vehicle, offers the finest long-range view of the Shivalik foothills and the Punjab plains extending to the horizon.</li><li>The Pasteur Institute, established in 1900 and still producing anti-rabies vaccine, is one of the cantonment's most historically significant institutions.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.8996,76.9672&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.8996,76.9672" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Kasauli Brewery</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Kasauli Brewery, established by Edward Abraham Dyer in 1820, is the oldest continuously operating brewery in Asia — predating Dyer's better-known Murree Brewery by 35 years.</li><li>Dyer came to Kasauli specifically because the Himalayan spring water and the cool climate were ideal for lager production, and the springs he identified still supply the brewery today.</li><li>The site, now owned by Mohan Meakin, produces the Solan No.1 lager brand in the original 1820 stone buildings.</li><li>Guided tours (bookable in advance) take visitors through the Victorian-era fermenting rooms, conditioning cellars and bottling hall — among the most atmospheric industrial heritage experiences in northern India.</li><li>The brewery is technically within the cantonment and operating day permits are required.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.9,76.965&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.9,76.965" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Chail</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>2,250 m</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Chail hill station, split between Solan and Shimla districts, was created in 1891 by Maharaja Bhupinder Singh of Patiala after the British banned him from Shimla following a dispute.</li><li>Determined to create a retreat superior to the summer capital, he acquired 5,500 acres of forest on the Shivalik ridge and built the Chail Palace at 2,250 m — now a heritage hotel managed by the Himachal Tourism Corporation.</li><li>The palace grounds contain the Chail Cricket Ground, built in 1893 at 2,444 m and Guinness-certified as the world's highest playing cricket ground, which still hosts matches.</li><li>The extensive forest sanctuary surrounding the palace complex contains pheasants, barking deer and leopard.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.9694,77.2014&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.9694,77.2014" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Dagshai</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,830 m</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Dagshai Cantonment, established in 1847 in the Shivalik foothills at 1,830 m, holds a darker chapter of colonial history: the Dagshai prison held British soldiers convicted of mutiny and Irish political prisoners in the late 19th century.</li><li>The cantonment jail, now converted to a military heritage museum (accessible with permission), documents this little-known history.</li><li>The stone church, cemetery and garrison buildings are scenicly maintained.</li><li>The surrounding deodar forest contains several Himalayan bird species, and the ridge trail offers views toward both the Kasauli ridge and the Shimla hills.</li><li>Dagshai sees very few visitors beyond cantonment residents and military historians.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.8564,76.9856&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.8564,76.9856" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Subathu</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,200 m</span><span>45 km from Shimla on the old hill road and sees almost no tourism</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>At 1,200 m in the Shivalik foothills, Subathu holds the distinction of being one of the oldest British military cantonments in northern India, established in 1815 immediately after the Anglo-Gurkha War.</li><li>The Gurkha Regimental Centre has been headquartered here since that date, making Subathu an important institution in the history of the Indian Army.</li><li>The cantonment church (St.</li><li>Andrew's, 1835), the officers' mess and the regimental museum are architectural and historical landmarks.</li><li>The surrounding secondary forest has good birdwatching.</li><li>Subathu is 45 km from Shimla on the old hill road and sees almost no tourism.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.9764,76.9436&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.9764,76.9436" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Solan Town</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,467 m</span><span>1 km path</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>At 1,467 m on the Kalka-Shimla road and railway, Solan is the district headquarters and the largest market town in the region.</li><li>The Shoolini Mata temple on the ridge above town, accessible by a 1 km path, is the presiding deity of the town and the site of the annual Shoolini Mela (June).</li><li>Solan's claim to the title 'Mushroom City of India' is well-founded: the Solan Mushroom Research Centre (established 1961) pioneered commercial mushroom cultivation in India, and button mushroom farms dot the hillsides around the town.</li><li>The town also sits within the Baddi-Barotiwala-Nalagarh pharmaceutical and industrial belt, Himachal's largest industrial zone.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.9044,77.0967&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.9044,77.0967" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Kumarhatti</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,400 m</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>On the old Hindustan-Tibet Road between Solan and Shimla, Kumarhatti at 1,400 m is a pleasant wayside settlement with government-run horticultural nurseries and experimental orchards that supply grafted apple, peach and apricot stock to farmers across Himachal.</li><li>The gardens are open to visitors and display an impressive range of temperate fruit varieties and ornamental species.</li><li>The Kandaghat valley below Kumarhatti, with the Ashwini Khud stream flowing through terraced farmland, is a quiet pastoral landscape suitable for half-day walks.</li><li>The Kalka-Shimla toy train stops at Kumarhatti station, making it a good base for a day trip on the railway.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.8833,77.0333&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.8833,77.0333" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Kandaghat</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,400 m</span><span>3 km</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Kandaghat station on the UNESCO-listed Kalka-Shimla narrow-gauge railway ranks among the most scenically situated stops on the line — a forest clearing at 1,400 m where the train slows to allow passengers a view of a small waterfall and the oak and rhododendron forest.</li><li>The nearby area is increasingly known for weekend farmstays and orchards.</li><li>The Ashwini Khud nature trail beginning near the station follows a seasonal stream through mixed forest for 3 km, making it a good short walk for day visitors arriving by toy train.</li><li>The Kandaghat–Solan road offers a pleasant cycling route through settled Himachali villages.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.9667,77.1&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.9667,77.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Shoolini Temple</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1 km path (or new ropeway) from the main bazaar</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The principal deity of Solan town, Goddess Shoolini is worshipped on a hilltop temple accessible via a 1 km path (or new ropeway) from the main bazaar.</li><li>The annual Shoolini Mela in June is the district's major cultural event, a three-day celebration that includes folk performances, crafts markets and religious processions drawing tens of thousands of participants.</li><li>The temple hilltop offers a panoramic view of the Solan valley, the Kasauli ridge, and on clear winter days the plains of Haryana.</li><li>A smaller shrine to Durga Mata adjacent to the main temple is considered particularly efficacious by local devotees.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.9044,77.0967&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.9044,77.0967" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Barog</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,560 m</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>Barog Station at 1,560 m on the Kalka-Shimla Railway is associated with one of India's most unusual engineering ghost stories: the British engineer Colonel Barog miscalculated the alignment for the tunnel being named after him, resulting in the two ends failing to meet.</li><li>Disgraced, he died by suicide at the site, and the railway was rerouted through a corrected tunnel design.</li><li>The station that bears his name is now a popular halt for toy train passengers — the forest rest house adjacent to the platform ranks among the most atmospheric heritage accommodation options on the line.</li><li>The surrounding Silver Fir Forest (Chil Van) is excellent for birdwatching, with Himalayan woodpeckers, nuthatches and minivets resident throughout the year.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.9331,77.0486&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.9331,77.0486" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div></div></div>
+  <div class="section" id="places"><h2>Top Places to Visit (10)</h2><div class="places-grid"><div class="place-card"><img src="https://drive.google.com/thumbnail?id=1c3mqiXspZk0DLJiX4Apu-ksWzaD5H8bs&sz=w600" alt="Kasauli" loading="lazy"><h3>Kasauli</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>2,455 m</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>Established in 1842 as a British military sanitarium, Kasauli is among the best-preserved hill cantonments in India — a living museum of colonial Himachal where the architecture, the tree-lined lanes and even the pace of life seem largely unchanged from the late Victorian era.</li><li>The Kasauli Cantonment Board maintains strict rules: no vehicles are permitted in the inner Mall area, no new construction can exceed the old style, and the original colonial bungalows must be maintained.</li><li>Monkey Point at the cantonment's southern tip (2,455 m), accessible on foot or in a cantonment vehicle, offers the finest long-range view of the Shivalik foothills and the Punjab plains extending to the horizon.</li><li>The Pasteur Institute, established in 1900 and still producing anti-rabies vaccine, is one of the cantonment's most historically significant institutions.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.8996,76.9672&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.8996,76.9672" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Kasauli Brewery</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Kasauli Brewery, established by Edward Abraham Dyer in 1820, is the oldest continuously operating brewery in Asia — predating Dyer's better-known Murree Brewery by 35 years.</li><li>Dyer came to Kasauli specifically because the Himalayan spring water and the cool climate were ideal for lager production, and the springs he identified still supply the brewery today.</li><li>The site, now owned by Mohan Meakin, produces the Solan No.1 lager brand in the original 1820 stone buildings.</li><li>Guided tours (bookable in advance) take visitors through the Victorian-era fermenting rooms, conditioning cellars and bottling hall — among the most atmospheric industrial heritage experiences in northern India.</li><li>The brewery is technically within the cantonment and operating day permits are required.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.9,76.965&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.9,76.965" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Chail</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>2,250 m</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Chail hill station, split between Solan and Shimla districts, was created in 1891 by Maharaja Bhupinder Singh of Patiala after the British banned him from Shimla following a dispute.</li><li>Determined to create a retreat superior to the summer capital, he acquired 5,500 acres of forest on the Shivalik ridge and built the Chail Palace at 2,250 m — now a heritage hotel managed by the Himachal Tourism Corporation.</li><li>The palace grounds contain the Chail Cricket Ground, built in 1893 at 2,444 m and Guinness-certified as the world's highest playing cricket ground, which still hosts matches.</li><li>The extensive forest sanctuary surrounding the palace complex contains pheasants, barking deer and leopard.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.9694,77.2014&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.9694,77.2014" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Dagshai</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,830 m</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Dagshai Cantonment, established in 1847 in the Shivalik foothills at 1,830 m, holds a darker chapter of colonial history: the Dagshai prison held British soldiers convicted of mutiny and Irish political prisoners in the late 19th century.</li><li>The cantonment jail, now converted to a military heritage museum (accessible with permission), documents this little-known history.</li><li>The stone church, cemetery and garrison buildings are scenicly maintained.</li><li>The surrounding deodar forest contains several Himalayan bird species, and the ridge trail offers views toward both the Kasauli ridge and the Shimla hills.</li><li>Dagshai sees very few visitors beyond cantonment residents and military historians.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.8564,76.9856&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.8564,76.9856" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Subathu</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,200 m</span><span>45 km from Shimla on the old hill road and sees almost no tourism</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>At 1,200 m in the Shivalik foothills, Subathu holds the distinction of being one of the oldest British military cantonments in northern India, established in 1815 immediately after the Anglo-Gurkha War.</li><li>The Gurkha Regimental Centre has been headquartered here since that date, making Subathu an important institution in the history of the Indian Army.</li><li>The cantonment church (St.</li><li>Andrew's, 1835), the officers' mess and the regimental museum are architectural and historical landmarks.</li><li>The surrounding secondary forest has good birdwatching.</li><li>Subathu is 45 km from Shimla on the old hill road and sees almost no tourism.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.9764,76.9436&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.9764,76.9436" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Solan Town</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,467 m</span><span>1 km path</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>At 1,467 m on the Kalka-Shimla road and railway, Solan is the district headquarters and the largest market town in the region.</li><li>The Shoolini Mata temple on the ridge above town, accessible by a 1 km path, is the presiding deity of the town and the site of the annual Shoolini Mela (June).</li><li>Solan's claim to the title 'Mushroom City of India' is well-founded: the Solan Mushroom Research Centre (established 1961) pioneered commercial mushroom cultivation in India, and button mushroom farms dot the hillsides around the town.</li><li>The town also sits within the Baddi-Barotiwala-Nalagarh pharmaceutical and industrial belt, Himachal's largest industrial zone.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.9044,77.0967&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.9044,77.0967" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Kumarhatti</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,400 m</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>On the old Hindustan-Tibet Road between Solan and Shimla, Kumarhatti at 1,400 m is a pleasant wayside settlement with government-run horticultural nurseries and experimental orchards that supply grafted apple, peach and apricot stock to farmers across Himachal.</li><li>The gardens are open to visitors and display an impressive range of temperate fruit varieties and ornamental species.</li><li>The Kandaghat valley below Kumarhatti, with the Ashwini Khud stream flowing through terraced farmland, is a quiet pastoral landscape suitable for half-day walks.</li><li>The Kalka-Shimla toy train stops at Kumarhatti station, making it a good base for a day trip on the railway.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.8833,77.0333&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.8833,77.0333" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Kandaghat</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,400 m</span><span>3 km</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Kandaghat station on the UNESCO-listed Kalka-Shimla narrow-gauge railway ranks among the most scenically situated stops on the line — a forest clearing at 1,400 m where the train slows to allow passengers a view of a small waterfall and the oak and rhododendron forest.</li><li>The nearby area is increasingly known for weekend farmstays and orchards.</li><li>The Ashwini Khud nature trail beginning near the station follows a seasonal stream through mixed forest for 3 km, making it a good short walk for day visitors arriving by toy train.</li><li>The Kandaghat–Solan road offers a pleasant cycling route through settled Himachali villages.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.9667,77.1&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.9667,77.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Shoolini Temple</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1 km path (or new ropeway) from the main bazaar</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The principal deity of Solan town, Goddess Shoolini is worshipped on a hilltop temple accessible via a 1 km path (or new ropeway) from the main bazaar.</li><li>The annual Shoolini Mela in June is the district's major cultural event, a three-day celebration that includes folk performances, crafts markets and religious processions drawing tens of thousands of participants.</li><li>The temple hilltop offers a panoramic view of the Solan valley, the Kasauli ridge, and on clear winter days the plains of Haryana.</li><li>A smaller shrine to Durga Mata adjacent to the main temple is considered particularly efficacious by local devotees.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.9044,77.0967&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.9044,77.0967" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Barog</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>1,560 m</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>Barog Station at 1,560 m on the Kalka-Shimla Railway is associated with one of India's most unusual engineering ghost stories: the British engineer Colonel Barog miscalculated the alignment for the tunnel being named after him, resulting in the two ends failing to meet.</li><li>Disgraced, he died by suicide at the site, and the railway was rerouted through a corrected tunnel design.</li><li>The station that bears his name is now a popular halt for toy train passengers — the forest rest house adjacent to the platform ranks among the most atmospheric heritage accommodation options on the line.</li><li>The surrounding Silver Fir Forest (Chil Van) is excellent for birdwatching, with Himalayan woodpeckers, nuthatches and minivets resident throughout the year.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=30.9331,77.0486&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=30.9331,77.0486" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div></div></div>
