-  <div class="section" id="places"><h2>Top Places to Visit (10)</h2><div class="places-grid"><div class="place-card"><h3>Chintpurni Temple</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>920 m</span><span>52 km) through the Shivalik forest is now well-maintained</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Chintpurni Devi temple at 920 m in the Shivalik foothills is one of the 51 Shakti Peethas — sacred sites associated with the body of the goddess Sati — and is among the most heavily visited religious sites in Himachal Pradesh, receiving an estimated 7–10 million pilgrims annually from Punjab, Haryana, Himachal and Jammu.</li><li>The goddess Chinnamastika, the headless form of Durga, is venerated here with particular intensity during the Navratri festivals (March–April and September–October).</li><li>The approach road from Una (52 km) through the Shivalik forest is now well-maintained.</li><li>Enormous prasad preparations — particularly kheer and halwa — and the constant flow of devotional music give the temple precinct a vibrantly intense devotional atmosphere.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.5417,76.3&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.5417,76.3" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Amb Temples</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>20 km from Una town near the village of Amb</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Amb temple complex, 20 km from Una town near the village of Amb, is a group of early medieval Hindu temples dating to the 8th–9th century in the Pratihara style — remarkable because they are among the finest and best-preserved ancient stone temples in the outer Himalayan belt, and yet virtually unknown outside academic circles.</li><li>The main shikhara temples, built in the nagara (north Indian) style with carved exteriors depicting Vishnu, Shiva and their attendants, are protected by the Archaeological Survey of India.</li><li>The site is set in agricultural land with a pleasant rural character.</li><li>The quality of the stone carving — particularly the doorway guardians and the river goddesses Ganga and Yamuna — is comparable to more famous temple sites of the period.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.68,76.12&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.68,76.12" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Una Town</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>369 m</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The district headquarters of Una at 369 m is the flattest and warmest point in Himachal Pradesh — a commercial and administrative centre in the foothills that serves as the main entry point into the state from Punjab.</li><li>The Swan river, channelised through the town, now includes into a linear river park with walking paths and gardens.</li><li>The Una railway station on the Pathankot-Una Himachal Railway line connects the district directly to the Punjab rail network.</li><li>Una's industrial character, dominated by the pharmaceutical factories of the Mehatpur and Bharwain industrial estates, gives it a distinctly different atmosphere from the mountain districts of Himachal.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.4681,76.2706&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.4681,76.2706" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Dera Baba Rudra Nand</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The ashram of the Shaivite saint Baba Rudra Nand on the outskirts of Una town is an active spiritual centre in the Nath tradition — the ancient yogic lineage associated with Guru Gorakhnath.</li><li>The ashram conducts regular satsang (spiritual gatherings), yoga classes and meditation programmes that are open to visitors.</li><li>The main temple is an attractive structure set in planted gardens.</li><li>The ashram also maintains a charitable school and health clinic serving surrounding villages.</li><li>Visitors are welcome with appropriate respect for the spiritual atmosphere; the institution maintains a simple, non-commercial character despite its proximity to the busy Una town.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.52,76.28&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.52,76.28" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Swan River Eco Park</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>3 km</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Swan river, a seasonal flashflood-prone stream draining the Shivalik foothills into the Punjab plains, now includes in Una into a linear riverside park where channelisation work and bank planting have created a green corridor through the town.</li><li>The eco park section, stretching approximately 3 km, has walking and cycling paths, bird observation points and seasonal gardens.</li><li>In winter (November–February) the river's edge attracts migratory birds including various egret species, kingfishers and lapwings.</li><li>The park is a popular local amenity for Una town residents and a pleasant space for early morning walks in the cooler months.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.47,76.27&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.47,76.27" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Nangal Dam</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The Nangal Dam on the Satluj river, just south of the HP-Punjab border near Nangal town, is the downstream component of the Bhakra-Nangal project — India's largest post-Independence irrigation and hydro project, inaugurated by Nehru in 1963.</li><li>The Nangal reservoir stretches back toward Bilaspur and forms the lower basin of Gobind Sagar.</li><li>The dam structure itself, significantly smaller than the Bhakra main dam, can be visited with security clearance; the township built for project engineers retains its planned 1950s character.</li><li>The reservoir margins on the HP side have good birdlife, particularly in winter when the Bhakra-Nangal reservoir system attracts bar-headed geese, Eurasian wigeons and various diving ducks.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.4,76.3833&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.4,76.3833" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Kutlehar Fort</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The ruined fort of the former Kutlehar princely state — one of the 30+ small principalities that dotted the Shivalik foothills before independence — stands on a forested hillside above the Una valley, its stone walls and corner bastions now largely consumed by vegetation.</li><li>The Kutlehar state, which managed to maintain autonomy through the British period, acceded to India in 1948; its former palace in the village below has been converted to private use.</li><li>The climb to the fort summit through secondary forest takes approximately 30 minutes and offers views over the Una valley floor and the Swan river floodplain.</li><li>The site is not formally developed for tourism and is visited primarily by local history enthusiasts.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.65,76.2&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.65,76.2" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Sathali Devi Temple</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>6 km from Chintpurni</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>A small but ancient goddess temple on a forested ridge 6 km from Chintpurni, Sathali Devi is venerated as a complementary deity to the Chintpurni goddess — many pilgrims to Chintpurni include Sathali in their circuit.</li><li>The ridge on which the temple sits offers views over the Shivalik foothills and the Una valley that are particularly scenic in the early morning light.</li><li>The surrounding deodar forest, relatively intact because of the religious protection of the temple catchment, supports good woodland birdlife.</li><li>The temple is tended by a hereditary priest family from the adjacent village.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.58,76.32&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.58,76.32" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Bangana Craft Village</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>A rural cluster in the border zone between Una and Hamirpur districts, Bangana and its surrounding villages are known for traditional Himachali textile weaving — particularly the production of heavy wool pattus (shawls) and blankets using horizontal frame looms that are still operated in domestic settings.</li><li>A Himachal Pradesh government crafts development initiative has supported a small weavers' cooperative that welcomes visitors and sells directly.</li><li>The weaving process — carding, spinning and slow shuttle weaving — can be observed in family homes with advance notice through the cooperative.</li><li>The products, particularly the heavy natural-dyed blankets, are significantly cheaper here than in urban craft emporiums.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.7833,76.4&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.7833,76.4" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Pong Dam Wildlife Sanctuary</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The 307 sq km Pong Dam Wetland, created by the impounding of the Beas river at Pong on the HP-Punjab border, is a Ramsar Wetland of International Importance and among the most significant waterfowl wintering grounds in the western Himalaya.</li><li>The reservoir, at its peak in November–February, hosts up to 100,000 bar-headed geese — a single population of this high-altitude migratory species that breeds on the Tibetan plateau and winters here.</li><li>Over 220 species of waterbirds have been recorded including greylag goose, common crane, tufted duck, ferruginous duck and various tern species.</li><li>The Wildlife Department maintains boat-based viewing safaris from the Nagrota Surian entry point on the HP shore.</li><li>Winter mornings when the geese lift off the water in enormous flocks are among the great wildlife spectacles of northern India.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.95,75.95&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.95,75.95" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div></div></div>
