er when the Bhakra-Nangal reservoir system attracts bar-headed geese, Eurasian wigeons and various diving ducks.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.4,76.3833&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.4,76.3833" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><img src="https://drive.google.com/thumbnail?id=1g1Y_sjMe-UDoJBfNJhFNl7GTXmCn1uRB&sz=w600" alt="Kutlehar Fort" loading="lazy"><h3>Kutlehar Fort</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The ruined fort of the former Kutlehar princely state — one of the 30+ small principalities that dotted the Shivalik foothills before independence — stands on a forested hillside above the Una valley, its stone walls and corner bastions now largely consumed by vegetation.</li><li>The Kutlehar state, which managed to maintain autonomy through the British period, acceded to India in 1948; its former palace in the village below has been converted to private use.</li><li>The climb to the fort summit through secondary forest takes approximately 30 minutes and offers views over the Una valley floor and the Swan river floodplain.</li><li>The site is not formally developed for tourism and is visited primarily by local history enthusiasts.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.65,76.2&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.65,76.2" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Sathali Devi Temple</h3><div class="place-tag-row"><span>6 km from Chintpurni</span></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>A small but ancient goddess temple on a forested ridge 6 km from Chintpurni, Sathali Devi is venerated as a complementary deity to the Chintpurni goddess — many pilgrims to Chintpurni include Sathali in their circuit.</li><li>The ridge on which the temple sits offers views over the Shivalik foothills and the Una valley that are particularly scenic in the early morning light.</li><li>The surrounding deodar forest, relatively intact because of the religious protection of the temple catchment, supports good woodland birdlife.</li><li>The temple is tended by a hereditary priest family from the adjacent village.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.58,76.32&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.58,76.32" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Bangana Craft Village</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>A rural cluster in the border zone between Una and Hamirpur districts, Bangana and its surrounding villages are known for traditional Himachali textile weaving — particularly the production of heavy wool pattus (shawls) and blankets using horizontal frame looms that are still operated in domestic settings.</li><li>A Himachal Pradesh government crafts development initiative has supported a small weavers' cooperative that welcomes visitors and sells directly.</li><li>The weaving process — carding, spinning and slow shuttle weaving — can be observed in family homes with advance notice through the cooperative.</li><li>The products, particularly the heavy natural-dyed blankets, are significantly cheaper here than in urban craft emporiums.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.7833,76.4&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.7833,76.4" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div><div class="place-card"><h3>Pong Dam Wildlife Sanctuary</h3><div class="place-tag-row"></div><ul class="place-facts"><li>The 307 sq km Pong Dam Wetland, created by the impounding of the Beas river at Pong on the HP-Punjab border, is a Ramsar Wetland of International Importance and among the most significant waterfowl wintering grounds in the western Himalaya.</li><li>The reservoir, at its peak in November–February, hosts up to 100,000 bar-headed geese — a single population of this high-altitude migratory species that breeds on the Tibetan plateau and winters here.</li><li>Over 220 species of waterbirds have been recorded including greylag goose, common crane, tufted duck, ferruginous duck and various tern species.</li><li>The Wildlife Department maintains boat-based viewing safaris from the Nagrota Surian entry point on the HP shore.</li><li>Winter mornings when the geese lift off the water in enormous flocks are among the great wildlife spectacles of northern India.</li></ul><button type="button" class="place-map-btn" onclick="toggleMapBox(this)">🧭 View on Map</button><div class="map-box" style="display:none;margin-top:10px"><iframe width="100%" height="200" style="border:0;border-radius:8px;display:block" loading="lazy" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=31.95,75.95&z=15&output=embed"></iframe><a href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&destination=31.95,75.95" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="map-link">Open in Google Maps →</a></div></div></div></div>
